# Root-level ignores only.
#
# Every pattern here is deliberately anchored with a leading `/` so it matches at
# the repository root and nowhere else. That is the whole design of this file:
# `landing_page/.gitignore` and `tho_web/.gitignore` are unchanged from when each
# project was its own repository, and their patterns are already rooted
# (`/node_modules`, `/.next/`, `/out/`) — which git resolves against the directory
# containing the `.gitignore`, not the repository root. So they already cover
# `landing_page/node_modules` and `tho_web/.next` correctly.
#
# An unanchored pattern here would apply recursively and could shadow a negation
# in one of those files — `!.env.example`, which both rely on so a fresh clone has
# a template to copy. Anchoring keeps the three files from interacting at all.

# A root install, if anyone ever runs one. There is no npm workspace here (see
# README) so this should stay empty in normal use.
/node_modules
/package-lock.json

# Deploy CLI scratch, if either is ever linked from the repo root rather than
# from an app directory.
/.vercel
/.netlify

# Env files never belong at the root — each app owns its own, and each app's
# .gitignore already permits its own .env.example.
/.env*

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
/.idea/
*.swp

# The migration safety copies, if they are ever moved inside the repo. They hold
# a full mirror of both projects and must never be committed.
/_migration_backup/
